This is a logic question, so the right throwaway is a single HTML file you double-click, with free-play buttons and guided walkthroughs. I haven't seen your real state machine, so the transition rules below are my assumptions, and nothing here has been run.

## What to model

Paid-after-cancelled rarely comes from a user clicking a button. It comes from an asynchronous payment result landing after the cancel. The demo therefore models two things separately:

- **Reservation status:** `held`, `awaiting_payment`, `paid`, `cancelled`, `expired`.
- **Payments in flight:** started but not yet resolved, which you deliver whenever you choose, including after a cancel.

A policy switch controls what happens when a payment success arrives while the reservation is not `awaiting_payment`:

- **naive:** set `paid` regardless.
- **strict:** ignore the event.
- **refund:** ignore the event and queue a refund.

Two checks render after every action:

- **Order check:** does the history contain `paid` after `cancelled` or `expired`?
- **Money check:** has money been captured that is neither a live paid reservation nor a queued refund?

The second check matters because blocking the transition does not undo the charge.

## Walkthroughs

| Tab | Steps | What it probes |
|---|---|---|
| Happy path | start payment, success | Baseline |
| Late success after cancel | start payment, cancel, success | The core question |
| Late success after expiry | start payment, expire, success | Same race via timeout |
| Double pay | start payment twice, success, success | Second capture on a paid reservation |
| Cancel after paid | start payment, success, cancel | The legitimate order, for contrast |

## The file

Save it next to the reservation module as `reservation-state.PROTOTYPE.html`.

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>PROTOTYPE - reservation: paid after cancelled?</title>
<style>
  body{font:14px system-ui;max-width:860px;margin:2em auto}
  button{margin:2px;padding:6px 10px}
  .on{font-weight:bold;border-bottom:3px solid #000}
  pre{background:#f4f4f4;padding:10px}
  .bad{background:#fdd;padding:8px}.ok{background:#dfd;padding:8px}
</style>
<h2>PROTOTYPE (throwaway): can a reservation reach paid after cancelled?</h2>
<p>Late payment success policy:
  <select id="policy" onchange="render()">
    <option value="naive">naive: always set paid</option>
    <option value="strict">strict: ignore</option>
    <option value="refund">refund: ignore and queue refund</option>
  </select></p>
<div id="tabs"></div>
<div id="controls"></div>
<div id="verdict"></div>
<pre id="state"></pre>
<pre id="log"></pre>
<script>
const fresh = () => ({status:'held', inflight:0, captured:0, refundsOwed:0, history:['held']});
let s = fresh(), log = [], tab = 'Free play', step = 0;
const set = st => { s.status = st; s.history.push(st); };
const open = () => ['held','awaiting_payment'].includes(s.status);

const actions = {
  startPayment() {
    if (!open()) return 'rejected';
    s.inflight++;
    if (s.status === 'held') set('awaiting_payment');
  },
  cancel() {
    if (['cancelled','expired'].includes(s.status)) return 'rejected';
    if (s.status === 'paid') s.refundsOwed++;
    set('cancelled');
  },
  expire() {
    if (!open()) return 'rejected';
    set('expired');
  },
  paymentSucceeded() {
    if (!s.inflight) return 'rejected: nothing in flight';
    s.inflight--; s.captured++;
    if (s.status === 'awaiting_payment') { set('paid'); return; }
    const p = document.getElementById('policy').value;
    if (p === 'naive') { set('paid'); return 'applied blindly'; }
    if (p === 'refund') { s.refundsOwed++; return 'ignored, refund queued'; }
    return 'ignored';
  },
  paymentFailed() {
    if (!s.inflight) return 'rejected: nothing in flight';
    s.inflight--;
    if (s.status === 'awaiting_payment' && !s.inflight) set('held');
  }
};

const scripts = {
  'Free play': null,
  'Happy path': ['startPayment','paymentSucceeded'],
  'Late success after cancel': ['startPayment','cancel','paymentSucceeded'],
  'Late success after expiry': ['startPayment','expire','paymentSucceeded'],
  'Double pay': ['startPayment','startPayment','paymentSucceeded','paymentSucceeded'],
  'Cancel after paid': ['startPayment','paymentSucceeded','cancel']
};

function run(name) {
  const before = s.status, note = actions[name]();
  log.push(`${name}: ${before} -> ${s.status}${note ? ' (' + note + ')' : ''}`);
  render();
}
function reset() { s = fresh(); log = []; step = 0; render(); }
function pick(t) { tab = t; reset(); }
function next() { run(scripts[tab][step++]); }

function render() {
  document.getElementById('tabs').innerHTML = Object.keys(scripts).map(t =>
    `<button class="${t === tab ? 'on' : ''}" onclick="pick('${t}')">${t}</button>`).join('');
  const sc = scripts[tab];
  document.getElementById('controls').innerHTML = (sc
    ? `<p>Script: ${sc.map((a, i) => i === step ? '<b>[' + a + ']</b>' : a).join(' → ')}</p>` +
      (step < sc.length ? '<button onclick="next()">Next step</button>' : '<i>done</i> ')
    : Object.keys(actions).map(a => `<button onclick="run('${a}')">${a}</button>`).join('')
  ) + '<button onclick="reset()">Reset</button>';

  const dead = s.history.findIndex(x => x === 'cancelled' || x === 'expired');
  const violated = dead >= 0 && s.history.indexOf('paid', dead) >= 0;
  const orphan = s.captured - s.refundsOwed - (s.status === 'paid' ? 1 : 0);
  document.getElementById('verdict').innerHTML =
    `<p class="${violated ? 'bad' : 'ok'}">Order check: ${violated ? 'PAID AFTER CANCELLED/EXPIRED' : 'ok'}</p>` +
    `<p class="${orphan > 0 ? 'bad' : 'ok'}">Money check: ${orphan > 0 ? orphan + ' capture(s) with no paid reservation and no refund' : 'ok'}</p>`;
  document.getElementById('state').textContent = JSON.stringify(s, null, 2);
  document.getElementById('log').textContent = log.join('\n');
}
render();
</script>
```

## What you should see

- **naive:** "Late success after cancel" trips the order check, so paid after cancelled is reachable.
- **strict:** the order check passes, but the money check fails because the customer was charged for a cancelled reservation.
- **refund:** both checks pass on the cancel and expiry races.
- **Double pay:** the money check fails under naive and strict, which shows the problem is wider than cancel.

## Before you trust the verdict

Replace the bodies of `actions` with your real transition rules. The answer for your system depends on two facts I assumed:

- Whether your payment-success handler checks the current status before writing `paid`.
- Whether cancel is allowed while a payment is in flight.

When it has answered the question, record the verdict on the implementation issue and keep the file on a throwaway branch, not main.

